  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. This investigation is an extension of the work done in \textit{Existence and multiplicity of solutions for the biharmonic Choquard equation} published in the Journal of Differential Equations, where similar research was conducted without the symmetry of the equation and without the term $\beta\Delta u$. Moreover, we demonstrate that the normalized ground state solution can be obtained in a minimal way when the nonlinearity is subcritical, and we also establish the uniqueness of the normalized ground state solution for the given equation when the nonlinearity is supercritical. Additionally, we derive an estimate of the convergence rate of the ground state solution when the nonlinearity is subcritical, and we also establish the existence of multiple solutions for the given equation when the nonlinearity is supercritical. Lastly, we discuss the particular case when the nonlinearity is $\delta$-type and investigate the existence of a normalized ground state solution for the given equation in the presence of a nonlocal term with a critical growth. As the equation is invariant under the symmetry operation, we can obtain a normalized ground state solution that achieves the least value of the modified functional, and as a result, we can establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for the given equation. Moreover, we can demonstrate that the normalized ground state solution can be obtained in a minimal way when the nonlinearity is subcritical, and we also establish the uniqueness of the normalized ground state solution for the given equation when the nonlinearity is supercritical. Additionally, we derive an estimate

